    "model_design": "Replace the current progressive unfreezing and multi-scale fusion (including PunctuationPatternEncoder, ArchaicVocabularyDetector, LayerAttention, and GatedCrossAttention) with LoRA applied to all query/value projection matrices in all 24 layers of DeBERTa-v3-large (rank=8, alpha=16, dropout=0.1). Keep only the classification head and remove all auxiliary modules to avoid overfitting. This allows simultaneous all-layer adaptation with only ~2M trainable parameters, directly addressing the underfitting caused by frozen backbone layers.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the current cosine annealing scheduler (stuck at 1.71e-05) with OneCycleLR (max_lr=5e-4 for LoRA params, 1e-3 for head, pct_start=0.1, anneal_strategy='cos'), and replace label smoothing cross-entropy with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, alpha=class-balanced). Keep data augmentation as synonym replacement (30% of content words via WordNet) applied inside __getitem__. This provides proper learning rate dynamics and focuses on hard examples, breaking the validation plateau."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm the plateau at 0.1006 log loss after epoch 29, validating the initial plan's root cause analysis. Similar historical experiments show that multi-view fusion and two-stage training successfully broke similar plateaus (successful experiment 2), while progressive unfreezing and layer-wise LR scheduling caused instability and overfitting (failed experiment 2). The initial plan proposes a three-pathway ensemble (fine-tuned DeBERTa, frozen DeBERTa with mean pooling, character CNN) with learned gating. However, to stay within runtime limits, I simplify to two pathways (fine-tuned DeBERTa with last-layer CLS, frozen DeBERTa with mean pooling) plus lightweight character CNN from the existing punctuation encoder. I also remove the bug-prone auxiliary feature pipeline (archaic vocabulary detector) per the initial plan, replacing it with gating that uses sentence-level structural features already computed (punctuation ratios, sentence length variance). The two-stage training avoids progressive unfreezing bugs seen in historical failures. Thus, modifications focus on model_design to implement the simplified multi-view fusion with gating, and training_evaluation to implement the two-stage schedule (gating+head warmup then full fine-tuning with cosine annealing). data_processing is not modified because the existing features are already engineered and suffice as gating inputs.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current StyleAwareClassifier with a MultiViewFusionClassifier that processes text through two parallel pathways: (1) a fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-large pathway using last-layer CLS pooling (as before, last 4 layers unfrozen), and (2) a frozen DeBERTa-v3-large pathway using mean pooling over all hidden layers (outputs.hidden_states stacked and mean-pooled to 1024-dim). Additionally, retain the existing PunctuationPatternEncoder (character CNN) as a third lightweight pathway for orthographic patterns. Each pathway outputs a 256-dim embedding via learned linear projections. A gating network (2-layer MLP with 128 hidden units) takes as input the existing handcrafted features (punctuation counts, sentence length, etc. from the dataframe) and produces softmax weights over the three pathways. The fused embedding is the weighted sum of pathway embeddings, then passed to the existing classifier head (hidden_size=1024+256? already 1024+128, but we'll concatenate gating input embedding too? No, the gating uses handcrafted features to compute weights only, not as input to classifier). The classifier head remains a 3-layer MLP (1024->512->256->3). Dropout rates set to 0.3. This architecture directly implements the initial plan's multi-view fusion without introducing excessive parameters (approx. 2M additional). The gating network uses the precomputed feature_columns as input (scaled properly). Interface: forward must accept the same arguments plus a 'stylistic_features' tensor; the model's backbone and components are preserved.", "training_evaluation": "Implement a two-stage training schedule as per initial plan. Stage 1 (epochs 0-4): freeze all backbone parameters (both DeBERTa pathways and CNN), train only the gating network and classifier head with a higher learning rate (5e-4). Stage 2 (epochs 5-24): unfreeze the fine-tuned DeBERTa pathway (last 4 layers) and train end-to-end with learning rate 1e-5, while the frozen DeBERTa pathway and CNN remain frozen. Use cosine annealing with a single cycle over Stage 2 (T_max=20 epochs, eta_min=1e-6). Remove warmup steps to avoid historical bugs; the two-stage starts with a warm gating mechanism. Apply gradient clipping (norm 1.0) and mixed precision (AMP). Early stopping patience 5 based on validation log loss, maximum 25 epochs total. This schedule avoids the oscillation instability seen in previous runs (epochs 4-5, 25-26) by first establishing reliable fusion weights then fine-tuning gradually. Save the best model based on validation log loss. Generate submission as before."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm the plateau at 0.1006 log loss after epoch 29, validating the initial plan's root cause analysis. Similar historical experiments show that multi-view fusion and two-stage training successfully broke similar plateaus (successful experiment 2), while progressive unfreezing and layer-wise LR scheduling caused instability and overfitting (failed experiment 2). The initial plan proposes a three-pathway ensemble (fine-tuned DeBERTa, frozen DeBERTa with mean pooling, character CNN) with learned gating. However, to stay within runtime limits, I simplify to two pathways (fine-tuned DeBERTa with last-layer CLS, frozen DeBERTa with mean pooling) plus lightweight character CNN from the existing punctuation encoder. I also remove the bug-prone auxiliary feature pipeline (archaic vocabulary detector) per the initial plan, replacing it with gating that uses sentence-level structural features already computed (punctuation ratios, sentence length variance). The two-stage training avoids progressive unfreezing bugs seen in historical failures. Thus, modifications focus on model_design to implement the simplified multi-view fusion with gating, and training_evaluation to implement the two-stage schedule (gating+head warmup then full fine-tuning with cosine annealing). data_processing is not modified because the existing features are already engineered and suffice as gating inputs.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current StyleAwareClassifier with a MultiViewFusionClassifier that processes text through two parallel pathways: (1) a fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-large pathway using last-layer CLS pooling (as before, last 4 layers unfrozen), and (2) a frozen DeBERTa-v3-large pathway using mean pooling over all hidden layers (outputs.hidden_states stacked and mean-pooled to 1024-dim). Additionally, retain the existing PunctuationPatternEncoder (character CNN) as a third lightweight pathway for orthographic patterns. Each pathway outputs a 256-dim embedding via learned linear projections. A gating network (2-layer MLP with 128 hidden units) takes as input the existing handcrafted features (punctuation counts, sentence length, etc. from the dataframe) and produces softmax weights over the three pathways. The fused embedding is the weighted sum of pathway embeddings, then passed to the existing classifier head (hidden_size=1024+256? already 1024+128, but we'll concatenate gating input embedding too? No, the gating uses handcrafted features to compute weights only, not as input to classifier). The classifier head remains a 3-layer MLP (1024->512->256->3). Dropout rates set to 0.3. This architecture directly implements the initial plan's multi-view fusion without introducing excessive parameters (approx. 2M additional). The gating network uses the precomputed feature_columns as input (scaled properly). Interface: forward must accept the same arguments plus a 'stylistic_features' tensor; the model's backbone and components are preserved.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement a two-stage training schedule as per initial plan. Stage 1 (epochs 0-4): freeze all backbone parameters (both DeBERTa pathways and CNN), train only the gating network and classifier head with a higher learning rate (5e-4). Stage 2 (epochs 5-24): unfreeze the fine-tuned DeBERTa pathway (last 4 layers) and train end-to-end with learning rate 1e-5, while the frozen DeBERTa pathway and CNN remain frozen. Use cosine annealing with a single cycle over Stage 2 (T_max=20 epochs, eta_min=1e-6). Remove warmup steps to avoid historical bugs; the two-stage starts with a warm gating mechanism. Apply gradient clipping (norm 1.0) and mixed precision (AMP). Early stopping patience 5 based on validation log loss, maximum 25 epochs total. This schedule avoids the oscillation instability seen in previous runs (epochs 4-5, 25-26) by first establishing reliable fusion weights then fine-tuning gradually. Save the best model based on validation log loss. Generate submission as before."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The trajectory shows a dramatic regression from 0.1006 to 0.3936 after the multi-view fusion attempt, indicating severe overfitting or training instability. The two-stage schedule failed; Stage 1 showed no improvement (gating+head stuck at ~1.09 loss), and Stage 2 improved but plateaued quickly, suggesting the gating mechanism introduced noise rather than signal. The core issue is over-parametrization with 869M parameters overwhelming the limited 14K training samples, causing the model to memorize rather than generalize. The successful 0.1006 result used a simpler single-backbone approach (StyleAwareClassifier with frozen DeBERTa + auxiliary features), confirming that complexity here hurts performance. Therefore, I must revert to a simpler, more regularized design: remove dual-pathway fusion and gating, use only a frozen DeBERTa backbone with a lightweight trainable head, and apply stronger regularization (dropout, weight decay, label smoothing) to prevent overfitting. This is a Tier 3 paradigm shift back to a simpler high-regularization approach.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Simplify architecture: replace MultiViewFusionClassifier with a single frozen DeBERTa-v3-large backbone (no fine-tuning, all parameters frozen) followed by a 2-layer MLP classifier head (1024 -> 256 -> 3) with higher dropout rates (0.5) and L2 regularization. Remove the gating network, character CNN, and auxiliary pathway projections entirely. This reduces trainable parameters from ~27M to ~265K, drastically lowering overfitting risk while leveraging powerful frozen representations. The model's forward method will only use input_ids and attention_mask; stylistic features are not used.", "training_evaluation": "Simplify training: single-stage training with AdamW, lr=1e-3 (higher because only head trains), weight_decay=0.1, and cosine annealing over 30 epochs (T_max=30). Use gradient clipping (norm=1.0) and label smoothing=0.1. Increase batch size to 32 for faster, more stable gradients. No warmup or two-stage schedule. Early stopping patience 7. Save best model and generate submission as before. This addresses the instability observed in the previous run."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The trajectory shows a dramatic regression from 0.1006 to 0.3936 after the multi-view fusion attempt, indicating severe overfitting or training instability. The two-stage schedule failed; Stage 1 showed no improvement (gating+head stuck at ~1.09 loss), and Stage 2 improved but plateaued quickly, suggesting the gating mechanism introduced noise rather than signal. The core issue is over-parametrization with 869M parameters overwhelming the limited 14K training samples, causing the model to memorize rather than generalize. The successful 0.1006 result used a simpler single-backbone approach (StyleAwareClassifier with frozen DeBERTa + auxiliary features), confirming that complexity here hurts performance. Therefore, I must revert to a simpler, more regularized design: remove dual-pathway fusion and gating, use only a frozen DeBERTa backbone with a lightweight trainable head, and apply stronger regularization (dropout, weight decay, label smoothing) to prevent overfitting. This is a Tier 3 paradigm shift back to a simpler high-regularization approach.",
